---------------------------\nHow our WhatsApp groups became my daily empathy dashboard | Shashikant Chaudhary posted on the topic | LinkedIn (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/shashikantchaudhary_communityfirst-happyeaters-foundersjourney-activity-7358816415950229504-4W_a)\nciteturn98search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; \n\n# How our WhatsApp groups became my daily empathy dashboard\n\nShashikant Chaudhary\n\n1y\n\nOur fourth Happy Eaters club just hit a milestone — and honestly, I’m still wrapping my head around the power of these micro-communities. 🎯 4500+ mothers 🟢 4 WhatsApp clubs 💬 Dozens of conversations daily — no prompts, no nudges What’s special? These parents don’t just use our platform… They shape it. They tell us what’s working, what’s confusing, what’s missing. They support each other in ways no product roadmap ever can. As a co-founder, this was the plot twist I didn’t expect. Our WhatsApp groups have become my daily dashboard of empathy. Thinking of starting a community? Don’t overthink it. Start small. Show up. Listen hard. And if you know a parent who could use a village — tag them. I’ll personally add them to one of our Happy Eaters clubs :) #CommunityFirst #HappyEaters #FoundersJourney #ParentingTogether #ListeningIsLearning\n\nSachchit Chaudhary💜\n\n1y\n\nThis cohort of the Happy Eaters universe has the most retention -Our community. 4 Clubs, 4500+ mothers. All of them just helping each other out day after day. This was the biggest surprise for me when we started Happy Eaters. Below is a screenshot of our fourth club :) Our small but powerful communities are what keep us going. The simple act of helping a fellow parent is the the glue that holds all these parents together. From a co-founder’s POV, not only has it helped me understand the sentiments of mothers and parents but has also served as a realtime feedback channel on the problems they face in their daily lives! If you needed a nudge to start your own community, let this be it. Go 